{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added a per-feed `refresh_interval` (integer minutes, 0 = use the global interval). Build and existing unit + integration tests pass; the new behaviour itself is not covered by any test I ran.\n\nWhat changed:\n- Migration: new `feeds.refresh_interval int not null default 0` column appended to the migration list.\n- Model/API: `refresh_interval` on the feed object and on the `PUT /v1/feeds/{feedID}` modification request (applied in `Patch`); also added to the Go `client` package's feed and modification-request types.\n- Storage: column is written by `CreateFeed`/`UpdateFeed` and read by the feed query builder.\n- Validation: values below 0 or above 527040 minutes (366 days) are rejected with a new `error.feed_invalid_refresh_interval` message. The upper bound is my addition.\n- Web UI: number input named `refresh_interval` in the network-settings section of the feed edit form, wired through `FeedForm`, the edit page and the update handler. Non-numeric input is treated as 0.\n- Scheduler: in `Feed.ScheduleNextCheck`, a positive per-feed interval replaces the globally computed one and is not clamped by the global min/max intervals.\n\nDecisions worth knowing:\n- A longer server-requested delay (Retry-After, TTL, Cache-Control, Expires) still wins over the per-feed interval, capped by the global maximum as before but never below the per-feed value. If the override should be absolute, that is a small change in `ScheduleNextCheck`.\n- Changing the interval does not reschedule the feed immediately; it takes effect from the next refresh.\n- The two new translation keys were added to all 24 locale files with English text, since the locale tests require every key in every language. They need real translations.\n- Feed creation requests (API and UI) do not accept `refresh_interval`; it can only be set by updating a feed.", "t": 1794463507.104}
{"tool": "_result", "name": "finish", "chars": 55, "t": 1794463507.104}
